1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:06,966
On the night of December fifth, in towns
across the Alps, a shaggy figure

2
00:00:06,966 --> 00:00:12,419
with horns, a long red tongue, and
rattling chains comes through the streets.

3
00:00:12,719 --> 00:00:14,555
His name is Krampus.

4
00:00:14,855 --> 00:00:19,135
He walks beside Saint Nicholas,
the kind bishop who brings gifts,

5
00:00:19,135 --> 00:00:22,278
and he has come
for the children who misbehaved.

6
00:00:22,578 --> 00:00:26,127
People often say that Krampus
is far older than

7
00:00:26,127 --> 00:00:29,522
Christmas, an ancient
pagan spirit of winter.

8
00:00:29,822 --> 00:00:32,779
But what do the oldest
records actually show?

9
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:38,289
In this episode, we follow
Krampus from a bishop's legend,

10
00:00:38,289 --> 00:00:43,040
to the devils of old Nicholas
customs, to a wave of colorful

11
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:48,031
postcards, and finally to the question
of how old he really is.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,677
Krampus begins with his companion.

13
00:00:57,977 --> 00:01:01,542
Saint Nicholas is said
to have been a bishop of Myra,

14
00:01:01,542 --> 00:01:04,559
in what is now Turkey,
in the fourth century.

15
00:01:04,859 --> 00:01:08,423
Almost nothing about his life
is known for certain.

16
00:01:08,723 --> 00:01:11,221
But his legends spread across Europe.

17
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:15,502
In one of the best known,
told in the thirteenth-century

18
00:01:15,502 --> 00:01:19,484
Golden Legend, Nicholas
secretly throws gold into a poor

19
00:01:19,484 --> 00:01:23,754
man's house at night, so
that his three daughters can marry.

20
00:01:24,054 --> 00:01:25,931
He does it three times.

21
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:33,280
Over time, the saint's
feast day, December sixth,

22
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:35,955
became linked with gifts for children.

23
00:01:36,255 --> 00:01:42,006
In some places, his visit also came with
a question: had the children been good?

24
00:01:42,306 --> 00:01:45,305
The giver of gifts had
also become a judge.

25
00:01:45,605 --> 00:01:50,007
And a judge, it seems, needed
someone to carry out the sentence.

26
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:58,359
When Nicholas's dark companions
appear in historical accounts,

27
00:01:58,359 --> 00:02:03,508
they belong to these Christian
customs: plays, and visits to homes.

28
00:02:03,808 --> 00:02:09,586
Nicholas arrives with figures dressed
as devils, in masks, furs, and bells.

29
00:02:09,886 --> 00:02:14,823
In these Christian settings, the devil
is brought under the saint's authority.

30
00:02:15,123 --> 00:02:18,303
Even the frightening
companion works for the saint.

31
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:22,503
The companions had many names.

32
00:02:22,803 --> 00:02:25,457
In much of Germany, Knecht Ruprecht.

33
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:29,017
In parts of Tyrol and Carinthia, Klaubauf.

34
00:02:29,317 --> 00:02:31,255
In Austria, Krampus.

35
00:02:31,555 --> 00:02:34,735
The origin of that name
is uncertain, though it

36
00:02:34,735 --> 00:02:38,261
is often linked to an old
word for a hook or a claw.

37
00:02:38,561 --> 00:02:43,116
The name, the costume, and the role
changed from valley to valley.

38
00:02:43,416 --> 00:02:45,806
There was never just one Krampus.

39
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:53,711
The Krampus most people picture today
owes a great deal to the postcard.

40
00:02:54,011 --> 00:02:58,623
In 1869, Austria-Hungary
introduced what is often

41
00:02:58,623 --> 00:03:02,658
called the world's
first official postcard.

42
00:03:02,958 --> 00:03:07,485
In the decades that followed, many
colorful cards for the Nicholas

43
00:03:07,485 --> 00:03:11,455
season were printed, and Krampus
appeared on many of them.

44
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:18,883
On these cards, Krampus took a familiar
shape: dark fur, horns, a long red tongue,

45
00:03:18,883 --> 00:03:24,605
chains, a bundle of twigs, and sometimes
children carried off on his back.

46
00:03:24,905 --> 00:03:27,168
Some cards were meant to frighten.

47
00:03:27,468 --> 00:03:30,828
Others turned him into
a striking piece of design.

48
00:03:31,128 --> 00:03:37,755
These cards, from a Vienna workshop around
1910, are now kept in a city museum.

49
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:45,704
So where does the idea
of a pagan Krampus come from?

50
00:03:46,004 --> 00:03:50,010
In the nineteenth century,
writers such as Jacob Grimm

51
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:54,395
looked for traces of a pre-Christian
past in later customs.

52
00:03:54,695 --> 00:03:59,419
Grimm wrote that he could well
imagine that even in heathen times,

53
00:03:59,419 --> 00:04:05,017
a god whose coming heralded a happy time
had a merry elf or dwarf at his side.

54
00:04:05,317 --> 00:04:08,281
He was imagining, not quoting a record.

55
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:12,371
Masked winter processions,
like the Perchten runs

56
00:04:12,371 --> 00:04:16,397
held around early January,
seemed to fit such ideas.

57
00:04:16,697 --> 00:04:20,238
And in some places, these
customs had already mixed

58
00:04:20,238 --> 00:04:23,779
with the Nicholas custom
by the eighteenth century.

59
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:32,215
It is possible that older winter
customs shaped the masks and furs.

60
00:04:32,515 --> 00:04:36,204
But no ancient text names
Krampus, and the accounts we

61
00:04:36,204 --> 00:04:39,755
have place him inside
the Christian Nicholas custom.

62
00:04:43,633 --> 00:04:46,646
In the twentieth century,
some critics questioned

63
00:04:46,646 --> 00:04:49,157
the use of Krampus to frighten children.

64
00:04:49,457 --> 00:04:51,335
Yet the custom lived on.

65
00:04:51,635 --> 00:04:55,565
Today, Krampus runs draw
large crowds in the Alps,

66
00:04:55,565 --> 00:05:00,698
and in recent decades he has found
new audiences far beyond them.

67
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:04,362
So, is Krampus older than Christmas?

68
00:05:04,662 --> 00:05:06,830
The records tell a different story.

69
00:05:07,130 --> 00:05:11,513
He appears as a devil in the service
of a saint, goes by many

70
00:05:11,513 --> 00:05:16,043
names in the Alps, and gets
his famous face from printed cards.

71
00:05:16,343 --> 00:05:18,327
His roots may run deeper.

72
00:05:18,627 --> 00:05:21,064
But as far as the sources can show,

73
00:05:21,064 --> 00:05:24,405
his story begins with a bishop
who brought gifts.

74
00:05:27,267 --> 00:05:29,594
Every story has an origin.

75
00:05:29,894 --> 00:05:34,203
Sometimes, it begins with a knock
at the door on a winter night.

76
00:05:35,133 --> 00:05:40,293
This video is based on historical
texts and public museum collections.

77
00:05:40,593 --> 00:05:43,313
The narration uses synthetic speech.

78
00:05:43,613 --> 00:05:47,569
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

79
00:05:47,869 --> 00:05:50,637
If you enjoyed this story, please like

80
00:05:50,637 --> 00:05:53,989
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

81
00:05:54,289 --> 00:05:56,041
Thank you for watching.
